details:
(a) the number of Fast Track Courts (FTCs) and Fast Track Special Courts (FTSCs) currently functional in the country, State-wise and Union Territory-wise;
(b) the number of pending cases in these courts as on date, along with the average conviction rate achieved by these courts during the last three years, State-wise; and
(c) the current number of vacancies at the level of judges and Special Public Prosecutors (SPPs) in these courts and the concrete steps being taken by Government to fill these vacancies speedily?

(a) the total number of vacant teaching posts in Central educational institutions including higher educational institutions, State-Government schools, and primary/secondary schools nationwide, categorized by State and Union Territory;
(b) the specific remedial steps and policy interventions Government has implemented over the last three years to bridge this deficit;
(c) whether Government has established a fixed timeline to achieve a standardized Pupil-Teacher Ratio (PTR) across all educational institutions; and
(d) the total financial assistance released to States under the Samagra Shiksha scheme specifically earmarked for teacher recruitment and training during this period?

(a) the specific steps taken by Government to improve and promote high-quality scientific research in the field of Ayush systems of medicine during the last three years;
(b) the total funds allocated, sanctioned and utilized for research and development (R&D) in Ayush institutions, council-wise, during the said period;
(c) whether Government has established any international collaborations or partnerships for clinical trials and scientific validation of Ayush drugs, if so, the details thereof; and
(d) the details of the institutional mechanisms put in place to integrate modern scientific tools with traditional Ayush knowledge to enhance global acceptance?

(a) whether Government has conducted any assessment of the impact of recent global geopolitical tensions and maritime transit risks on India's crude oil and natural gas supply chains;
(b) if so, the details of the vulnerabilities identified, particularly concerning major shipping lanes and choke points;
(c) specific strategic and preventive steps taken by Government to diversify import sources and secure alternative transit routes to mitigate these disruptions; and
(d) current status and holding capacity of India’s Strategic Petroleum Reserves (SPRs), including plans for their immediate expansion to handle prolonged supply shocks?

(a) the current status on the first hydrogen-powered passenger train in the country;
(b) the specific environmental, operational, and economic advantages of hydrogen fuel cell trains compared to traditional diesel and electric locomotives;
(c) the comprehensive safety protocols, technical standards, and precautionary measures taken by the Government to mitigate risks associated with hydrogen storage, leakage, and high-pressure refueling infrastructure; and
(d) the details of the domestic manufacturing capacity being developed for green hydrogen fuel and the financial allocation made for this project?

(a) the land area currently classified as 'Jhudpi Jungle' (shrub forests) across six districts of the Vidarbha region in Maharashtra (Nagpur, Wardha, Bhandara, Gondia, Chandrapur and Gadchiroli);
(b) the current status of central Government clearances under the Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980;
(c) the steps being taken by the Ministry to fast-track the regularisation of public infrastructure (such as schools, hospitals and railway networks) and residential slums built on these lands; and
(d) the joint measures being coordinated with the State Government of Maharashtra to implement the standard operating procedures (SOPs) for data submission and forest reclassification?

(a) the total number and names of districts officially identified as Educationally Backward Districts (EBDs) in the country, State-wise;
(b) the details of the socio-religious and marginalized communities that exhibit the lowest literacy and school enrolment rates within these identified districts;
(c) the specific central schemes, budgetary allocations and financial utilization during the last three years aimed at improving educational infrastructure in these areas; and
(d) the targeted steps taken by Government to upgrade school facilities, digital infrastructure and teacher deployment to mitigate educational backwardness among these specific districts and communities?

(a) whether Government has any plans to introduce a comprehensive Universal Health Coverage scheme that integrates both inpatient and outpatient care, along side covering the complete cost of essential medicines for all citizens and;
(b) whether Government has taken note of the NITI Aayog (2021) report on 'Health Insurance for India’s Missing Middle', which highlights that 80-85 per cent of households are catastrophically impacted by outpatient medical expenses; and
(c) if so, the specific measures being taken to mitigate this financial shock and reduce the high burden of out-of-pocket expenses for non-hospitalized treatments?

(a) the specific phase-wise targets set for sewage treatment, riverfront development and industrial effluent management under the National Mission for Clean Ganga (NMCG);
(b) the concrete achievements made under these targets so far, including the number of operational Sewage Treatment Plants (STPs) and total treated capacity; and
(c) the strategic steps, monitoring mechanisms and financial allocations being implemented to ensure all pending targets are successfully achieved within the remaining timeline?

(a) the current status of India's official preparation, infrastructural assessment and budgeting for upcoming Commonwealth Games;
(b) whether Government has initiated a formal bidding process or submitted a Letter of Intent to the International Olympic Committee (IOC) to host the Olympic Games;
(c) the comprehensive roadmap, financial allocations and identification of host cities planned for these mega-sporting events; and
(d) the strategy detailed by Government to ensure elite athlete training and podium-finish preparations aligning with these hosting timelines?

(a) whether Government mandates a timeframe for convening the meetings of the Central Advisory Board of Education (CABE);
(b) the dates on which CABE meetings were held during the last five years, along with the outcomes of each meeting;
(c) whether Government recognizes that irregular meetings disrupt the institutional mechanism required for cooperative federalism and unified thinking between the Union and the State Governments on education policies, and;
(d) whether Government has any plan to take concrete measures to ensure that CABE meets at least once every year to address pressing educational reforms systematically; and
(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) whether Non-Communicable Diseases (NCDs) account for over 60 per cent of mortalities in the country, and the data on NCD deaths over the last three years, State-wise;
(b) the steps taken to correct the spending disparity between advanced hospital care and primary, preventive care;
(c) the budget allocated and utilized for public health awareness, early screening, and routine diagnostics at the primary level during this period; and
(d) whether Government plans to revamp the National Programme for Prevention and Control of Non-Communicable Diseases (NP-NCD) to mandate universal periodic screening at primary health centers?

(a) the total number of annual and special reports submitted by the National Commission for Minorities to Government since 2023, year-wise;
(b) the details of the main recommendations made in each of these reports regarding the welfare and protection of minority communities;
(c) whether all these reports have been laid before each House of Parliament along with a memorandum of action taken as required under Section 13 of the National Commission for Minorities Act, 1992; and
(d) the details of the major recommendations accepted and implemented by Government so far, and the reasons for the delay in implementing the remaining recommendations?

(a) whether any State Governments have raised operational, financial or implementation concerns regarding the newly introduced rural employment scheme;
(b) if so, the details of the specific grievances raised by the States, category-wise;
(c) whether there is a significant delay in the release of outstanding dues, including wage and material components, under the MGNREGS to various States;
(d) if so, the State-wise details of the pending dues along with the reasons for such delays; and
(e) the remedial action taken by the Central Government to address the concerns of the States and expedite the clearance of all outstanding payments?

(a) the key findings of the recent National Sample Survey 80th Round on Health and the Economic Survey 2025-26 regarding the overall State of public health, morbidity rates and out-of-pocket expenditure (OOPE) in the country;
(b) whether the survey highlights a significant epidemiological shift from infectious diseases toward non-communicable lifestyle illnesses like diabetes and hypertension post-30 years of age and if so, the steps being taken to address this; and
(c) the detailed financial allocation and expansion targets achieved under the Ayushman Bharat Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (AB-PMJAY) and the inclusion of senior citizens under the Ayushman Bharat Vay Vandana scheme?

(a) whether Government has conducted any assessment regarding recent bottlenecks, leakage or grain quality concerns under the Targeted Public Distribution System across various States;
(b) if so, the details of the measures and structural steps taken to modernize the public distribution mechanism and eliminate foodgrain wastages;
(c) the total foodgrain storage capacity currently available with the Food Corporation of India and other State agencies, State/Union Territory-wise; and
(d) the volume of foodgrains stored under Cover and Plinth (CAP) storage open to weather conditions, along with the estimated post-harvest or storage losses incurred during the last two years?

(a) the steps taken by Government to implement the Public Procurement Policy for Micro and Small Enterprises Order, with particular focus on mandatory procurement targets from entrepreneurs belonging to Scheduled Castes, Scheduled Tribes, Other Backward Classes and women;
(b) the total value and percentage of annual procurement made by Ministries, Departments and Public Sector Undertakings from SC, ST, OBC and women-owned enterprises during each of the last three years, year-wise and category-wise; and
(c) the tangible benefits, economic impacts and number of beneficiaries recorded among these marginalized entrepreneur communities as a direct result of this policy?

(a) State-wise and UT-wise implementation status of the Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM), including the total number of rural household tap water connections provided till date;
(b) whether Government has deployed inspection teams to investigate widespread complaints regarding poor infrastructure quality, financial irregularities, tender premium scams across States, if so, the details thereof;
(c) the details regarding punitive action—including suspensions, blacklisting, FIRs, and arrests taken following central reviews; and
(d) whether Government has paused, frozen, or restructured fund allocations to specific States pending Action-Taken-Reports on corruption complaints, and the details of the new scheme based funding model under JJM 2.0?

(a) whether the NITI Aayog in September, 2022, citing a World Bank report, noted that rising temperatures and shifting monsoon rainfall may cost India 2.8 per cent of its GDP;
(b) whether it is a fact that, as a result of climate change, living standards of almost half of the country’s population is likely to be adversely affected by 2050; and
(c) if so, the response of Government thereon and adaptation measures proposed in the vulnerable sectors like agriculture, fisheries, water resources, health and ecosystems?

(a) the latest data on and the factors that contribute to the prevalence of anemia among women and children, State-wise;
(b) whether it is a fact that undernutrition, particularly in children makes them more vulnerable to disease and death;
(c) if so, the details thereof;
(d) whether Government has any plan to deal with malnutrition on a mission mode with specified timelines; and
(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) whether Government has taken any steps toward the constitution of the National Integrated Flood Management Group (NIFMG), as recommended by the Parliamentary Standing Committee;
(b) if so, the details of its current functional status, if not, the specific reasons for not establishing it as a permanent apex body despite its proposed role in unifying national and State-level flood mitigation efforts; and
(c) manner in which the Central Government currently ensures high-level coordination with State Governments and international neighbours for flood management in the absence of a centralised group?

(a) whether it is a fact that the punctuality index of 77 per cent has been recorded in 2024-25 in Indian Railways, if so, the primary reasons for this poor punctuality;
(b) the zone-wise details of average delays and the number of trains running more than three hours late during the last two years;
(c) the total number of complaints received regarding on-board cleanliness and toilet hygiene on all relevant portals in the last two years; and
(d) the specific steps taken to enhance On-Board Housekeeping Services (OBHS) and ensure strict adherence to train schedules?

(a) whether the National Mission on High Yielding Seeds was announced in the Union Budget 2025-26 with an initial outlay of ?100 crore;
(b) if so, the reasons for the zero utilization of funds under this Mission according to the revised estimates for the financial year 2025-26;
(c) whether no allocation has been made for the Mission in Budget 2026-2027;
(d) if so, the reasons for providing no budgetary allocation;
(e) the current status of the Mission's core objectives; and
(f) whether Government has shelved the Mission or merged it with any existing schemes like the Sub-Mission on Seeds and Planting Materials?

(a) whether it is a fact that Government aims at achieving 100 per cent Gross Enrolment Ratio (GER) at all levels of school education;
(b) the details of GER at the primary education level, secondary and higher secondary grades;
(c) whether Government has identified the reasons for low GER especially at the secondary and higher secondary grades; and
(d) if so, the details thereof and steps taken by Government to ensure 100 per cent GER at all levels of school education?

(a) whether Government considers hiring or constructing godowns through warehousing corporations and private investors to be more cost-effective than direct construction by the FCI;
(b) if so, whether any assessment has been done to compare total rental liabilities with the long-term cost of ownership;
(c) if so, whether a three-member committee was constituted to study this matter;
(d) if so, the status of the study and timeline for its completion; and
(e) the steps taken by Government to address the lack of systematic analysis in assessing the cost efficiency of the current model?

(a) the details of the recommendations made by the NCM regarding the safety of life and protection of the dignity of persons belonging to minority communities; 
(b) whether NCM has submitted any suo-moto reports or specific representations to Government concerning incidents of violence or harassment against minorities during the last two years, and if so, the details thereof;
(c) the status of these recommendations and the steps taken by Government to implement them;
(d) whether Government is considering to grant constitutional status to the NCM;
(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) whether Government has assessed the adverse effects of accumulated municipal waste on track maintenance, specifically track corrosion, drainage blockage and passenger health and safety;
(b) if so, the details thereof and steps taken by Government to mitigate environment and cleanliness challenges on lands adjacent to railway tracks;
(c) whether a joint mechanism with urban local bodies has been established for regular waste collection; and
(d) if so, the details thereof and number of urban corridors currently covered under this mechanism?

(a) whether Government has identified the key structural and environmental challenges hindering the growth of tourism sector, including issues related to "last-mile" connectivity, skilled manpower shortages and the ecological carrying capacity of popular destinations;
(b) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken by Government to attract both domestic and foreign tourists, particularly through the development of archaeological sites, improvements in infrastructure in coastal/hilly destinations and conservation of national parks; 
(c) the status of ongoing schemes and their specific impacts; and
(d) whether Government proposes any new policy for certification of destinations to ensure global standards of cleanliness and sustainability?

(a) whether it is a fact that the dropout rate at secondary and higher secondary levels of education still continues to be quite significant;
(b) whether large number of adolescents are not attending the school because they have to supplement households’ income, attend to domestic chores or that they find education is not necessary;
(c) if so, the details thereof; and
(d) steps taken by Government to attract and retain out-of-school adolescents in secondary and higher secondary grades of school education?

(a) whether Government has conducted any study/survey to assess prevalence of blindness or visual impairment in the country;
(b) if so, the details of the study/survey and the estimated number of persons with blindness or visual impairment, State-wise;
(c) whether it is a fact that blindness and visual impairment still pose significant challenges in achieving universal eye health;
(d) if so, whether Government has any comprehensive and dedicated approach for effectively tackling the situation including focus on improving eye banking and corneal transplant; and
(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) the rationale behind having different rates of compensation paid to the next of kin in the eventuality of causalities in air accidents for international and domestic passengers;
(b) whether Government has any plans to introduce uniform rates of compensation for both international and domestic passengers; and
(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) the length of roads sanctioned and completed under different verticals of Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY);
(b) the mechanism put in place for maintenance of rural roads;
(c) whether it is a fact that between January and December, 2025 large number of roads inspected for maintenance work by the National Quality Monitors were found to be unsatisfactory;
(d) if so, the details thereof including action taken against those found responsible for sub-standard work;
(e) whether Government has any plans to provide funds to states for road maintenance; and
(f) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) whether it is a fact that within the Indian Olympic Association there are significant governance issues that are adversely affecting India’s bid to host 2036 Olympic Games;
(b) if so, the details thereof and plans to resolve them;
(c) whether sports in India have been afflicted with anti-doping failures, if so, the steps taken to strictly adhere to the international rules; and
(d) the details on the measures adopted by Government to significantly enhance India’s performance in sports disciplines included in Olympic Games?

(a) the estimated number of children requiring cochlear implant in the country, State-wise;
(b) the details of the scheme that provides for cochlear implant, including the financial assistance and the eligibility criteria to identify beneficiaries;
(c) number of beneficiaries covered under the scheme and the amount spent by Government during last five years, year-wise;
(d) whether Government plans for universal coverage for providing cochlear implants; and
(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) the details of the towns and cities that faced public health crises in 2025 and in the beginning of 2026 due to contaminated piped water;
(b) the number of casualties and persons hospitalized for consuming contaminated water and the compensation paid to them;
(c) whether Government has identified the reasons for contamination and fixed accountability for any lapses;
(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and
(e) the details on steps taken to ensure availability of safe drinking water?

(a) a comparative assessment of freight movement on railway and on road transport in the last five years, and the revenue generated in both sectors since 2019, year-wise;
(b) whether it is a fact that freight movement on railways is more economically and environmentally sustainable as compared to its movement on road;
(c) if so, the details on the reasons that lead to the preference of adopting road transport over rail-based movement of freight; and
(d) the steps taken by Government to enhance freight movement on railway network?

(a) whether meteor crater Lonar lake is facing an unlikely threat of massive rise in water level;
(b) if so, the details thereof; and
(c) the details of steps taken by Government for its conservation and also to protect its unique biodiversity and ancient monuments surrounding it?

(a) the details on the achievements made in the implementation of the new internship scheme of Government offering opportunities to one crore youth in top 500 companies in the years 2024-25 and 2025-26, so far;
(b) the details regarding the companies who have offered internships in 2024-25 and 2025-26; and
(c) the proportion of such interns from SCs, STs, OBCs, Minorities, Women and Persons with Disabilities?

(a) whether Government has prepared district-wise, remedial action plans for groundwater contamination in districts identified as hotspots in the CGWB's Annual Ground Water Quality Report, 2024;
(b) the number of Jal Jeevan Mission habitations located in districts where groundwater contamination exceeds permissible limits;
(c) the percentage of such habitations receiving treated or alternative safe water sources;
(d) whether districts with over 40 per cent Nitrate exceedance have been mapped against fertilizer consumption and cropping intensity data; and
(e) if so, corrective advisories or restrictions that have been introduced?

(a) the details regarding mandays created, persons employed, wages paid and assets created under Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) since 2020, State-wise and year-wise;
(b) whether any irregularities were found in the implementation of MGNREGS;
(c) if so, the details of such irregularities, and;
(d) whether any accountability was fixed and persons penalized for such irregularities; and
(e) if so, the details thereof State-wise?

(a) the total number of complaints received from farmers regarding non-payment or delayed payment of claims under the Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY) in the last five years, year-wise, State/UT-wise;
(b) whether Government has taken any steps to improve the accuracy and transparency of crop damage assessment under PMFBY, if so, the details thereof;
(c) whether Government is considering amendments to the scheme to address payment inconsistencies, improve accountability of insurance companies; and
(d) if so, the details of measures taken to restore farmer confidence in the crop insurance scheme?

(a) the number of postfacto or retrospective environmental clearances granted since 2021, year-wise and State/UT-wise;
(b) the details of penalties collected from such cases, if any;
(c) the year-wise and State/UT-wise details of projects granted clearance despite being flagged by the Ministry’s own Expert Appraisal Committees;
(d) whether Government has conducted any assessment of ecological and economic consequences of regularising such projects; and
(e) if so, the findings thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) the annual parental income limits for students belonging to the Scheduled Castes, Other Backward Classes, Denotified and Nomadic Tribes and Economically Weaker Sections;
(b) the latest data on the number of students who received scholarships along with the amount disbursed to students from each category;
(c) whether Government is considering to increase the annual parental income limits for the students from the deprived sections of our society; and
(d) if so, the details thereof including the criteria to be followed in increasing the income limits?

(a) whether excessive salt consumption has emerged as a critical health crisis in India, primarily driving a 'silent pandemic' of Hypertension and Cardiovascular diseases;
(b) if so, the details thereof and steps taken by Government to promote awareness about the ill-effects of excessive salt consumption;
(c) whether it is a fact that consumption of Low Sodium Salt Substitutes (LSS) can go a long way in combating this 'silent pandemic'; and
(d) if so, the action taken by Government to ensure accessibility and affordability of LSSs?

(a) the annual allocation for health research as a proportion to India’s GDP since 2021, year-wise;
(b) whether it is a fact that public funding still continues to be the primary source of fund for meeting the expenditure on health research in the country;
(c) if so, considering the global standards with respect to investment in health research and in view of the needs of research and innovation in the health sector in the country, whether Government will substantially increase the allocation on health research; and
(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) whether it is a fact that due to limited financial resources Urban Local Bodies (ULB) usually find it difficult to operate and maintain water treatment plants, sewerage networks and other existing assets and those created under Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation (AMRUT);
(b) whether Government has any plan to establish a dedicated financial mechanism for O&M funding to supplement ULBs efforts, particularly in smaller municipalities with limited revenue generating capacity; and
(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) whether it is a fact that several parts of India, particularly urban areas have high levels of pollution and if so, the details thereof;
(b) whether any study has been conducted to assess the adverse impact of pollution on public health, particularly among children, women and elderly;
(c) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken by Government to ensure a significant reduction in pollution levels; and
(d) the amount spent by Government under various schemes aimed at environment protection and pollution control?

(a) the number of agricultural households in the country and the average annual income of these households, State-wise since 2014; 
(b) whether it is a fact that more than 50 percent of income of agricultural households is from wages and animal husbandary; and
(c) if so, the details thereof and steps taken by Government to improve agricultural productivity and enhance the income of agricultural households?

(a) the total number of crimes recorded as per the recent NCRB Report particularly regarding murder, rape, kidnapping & abduction, rioting, atrocities against SCs & STs, lynching, accidental deaths, State-wise details thereof;
(b) details regarding the cases registered, acquittal, conviction rate and pendency for various crimes; and
(c) steps taken by Government to ensure that everyone in the country is able to live a life with dignity?

(a) the total outstanding loan on farmers in the country and the number of agricultural households in debt, State-wise;
(b) whether the Ministry has made any assessment to identify the reasons for the indebtedness of farmers;
(c) if so, the details thereof and the action taken by Government to ameliorate the condition of farmers;
(d) if not, the reasons thereon;
(e) whether Government has any plans to waive farm loans; and
(f) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) whether Government has any plans to timely eliminate shortage of skilled manpower in Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs) streamline the administrative processes and equip them with advanced/cutting edge technologies;
(b) if so, the details thereof; 
(c) whether it is a fact that there still remains a large number of districts without KVKs; and
(d) if so, the reasons therefor and the timeline, if any, for providing the uncovered districts with KVKs?

(a) whether Government is implementing pollution control measures in numerous cities, particularly the non-attainment and million-plus cities with chronically poor air quality;
(b) if so, the details thereof including the achievements made, so far, along with the timelines and goals set out to be achieved in each of the cities;
(c) whether any study has been conducted to assess the adverse impact of pollution on public health, particularly among children, women and elderly; and
(d) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken to improve air quality?

(a) the total losses incurred in the year 2025 due to floods, flash floods, cloudburst, landslides and heavy rainfall as per the assessment of the State Governments, State-wise;
(b) the details regarding the central teams deputed to assess the damages and their findings, State-wise; and
(c) details regarding the financial assistance provided for rebuilding of infrastructure including the houses damaged or washed away and compensation to farmers, State-wise?

(a) whether the Department-related Parliamentary Standing Committee on Education has, in its 370th Report, recommended the implementation of Article 15(5) of the Constitution of India and the provision of reservations for Scheduled Castes (SCs), Scheduled Tribes (STs), Other Backward Classes (OBCs) and Economically Weaker Sections (EWSs) in private Higher Educational Institutions (HEIs);
(b) if so, the details thereof;
(c) whether Government proposes to introduce any legislation during the current session to implement Article 15(5) and the reservation policy in private HEIs as per the recommendations of the Committee;
(d) if so, the details thereof; and
(e) if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) the details regarding stunting, wasting and overweight in children according to the latest National Family Health Survey (NHFS), State-wise;
(b) the details regarding prevalence of anaemia in children under 5, women between 15-49 years and men between 15-49 years as per the latest NHFS, State-wise; and 
(c) whether Government has taken any steps to address the issues of stunting, wasting, overweight and anaemia?

(a) whether high operational costs, infrastructure bottlenecks and persistent human resource shortages are some of the major challenges faced by the civil aviation sector in the country;
(b) if so, the details thereof and the response of Government thereon and;
(c) whether Government has any plans to make structural and administrative reforms in the sector to ensure safety in air travel; and
(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) whether it is a fact that a large number of farmers and their organisations have been demanding central legislation for providing a legal guarantee for Minimum Support Price (MSP) for all agricultural produce;
(b) whether Government will consider to enact a law which will provide such a legal guarantee; and
(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) whether it is a fact that several cities in the country are ranked among the most polluted in the world;
(b) whether severe air pollution poses significant health risks and economic costs;
(c) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken by Government to ensure a significant reduction in pollution levels; and
(d) the amount spent by Government under various schemes aimed at environment protection and pollution control?

(a) the salient provisions of “The Prohibition of Employment as Manual Scavengers and their Rehabilitation Act, 2013”;
(b) the number of cases registered for violating the provisions of this Act, State-wise;
(c) the number of persons prosecuted and the conviction rate under this Act during the last five years, State-wise and year-wise; and
(d) the amount of compensation paid for deaths and for disabilities resulting from sewer work during the last five years?

(a) the total number of sanctioned posts in CGHS dispensaries;
(b) whether it is a fact that a number of posts, both in Allopathic and Ayush dispensaries remain unfilled;
(c) if so, the details thereof;
(d) whether any steps have been taken promptly to fill up all the vacant positions; and
(e) if so, the details thereon?

(a) the target set by the National Health Policy in regard to the allocation of funds for health;
(b) the details regarding budget allocation on health in terms of BE as percentage of GDP (at Current Prices) since 2020-21 and its utilisation, year-wise;
(c) whether Government has any plans to enhance resources for the health sector for improving the health care to global standards and in proportion to the rate of population growth; and
(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) the details regarding the airports/ airstrips in the country under civil and defence administration;
(b) the recent data regarding the number of flights operated per day, the number of passengers and the volume of freight handled;
(c) whether Government has undertaken any comprehensive evaluation of the Civil Aviation Safety Environment;
(d) if so, the details of its findings;
(e) whether Government has taken any steps for reforming the administrative structure for ensuring safety in the civil aviation sector; and
(f) if so, the details thereof?

(a) the number of Ayushman Arogya Mandirs (AAMs) sanctioned and operational until March 2025, State-wise;
(b) the number of AAMs expected to be operational by March 2026;
(c) the number of AAMs providing digital services, district-wise and State-wise;
(d) the number of skilled birth attendants working in AAMs, district-wise and State-wise;
(e) the number of AAMs providing teleconsultation services, district-wise and State-wise; and
(f) the number of vacancies in AAMs, broken-down by types of health professionals, district-wise and State-wise?

(a) the total rooftop solar capacity (in megawatts) installed under Phase II of the Rooftop Solar Programme as of June 2025, against the targets originally set under the scheme; 
(b) the number of urban and rural households that have received subsidies under the programme, State-wise; 
(c) whether delays have been reported in subsidy disbursal or empanelment of vendors and if so, the reasons for such delays; and 
(d) the measures taken by the Ministry to simplify the application and installation process, especially for low-income and rental households and to promote wider adoption of rooftop solar?

(a) as of June, 2025, the number of villages and families in each State that have an operational faucet for water under the Jal Jeevan Mission; 
(b) details of the number of complaints received about defective faucets or insufficient water supply, district-wise and State-wise; and 
(c) details of steps being taken to address implementation gaps and ensure quality is maintained?

(a) the current deficit of affordable housing units in urban areas, city-wise and State-wise as of June, 2025;
(b) the total number of beneficiaries under the scheme as of June, 2025;
(c) the number of applications under review and pending;
(d) the number of units approved and finalised between 2024-25 under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana (PMAY);
(e) the measures being undertaken to address the deficit of housing units; and
(f) whether models of Public-Private Partnerships (PPP) are being explored; if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?

